#1217 is fixed and committed. With a broken .yoyo/memory.json, one REPL session now prints the "project memories not loaded" warning once per problem, not on every reload.

Results (built binary in a real terminal session, file containing just {, inputs /memories, /remember x, /memories, /quit):

| | warning lines |
|---|---|
| Before | 4 (startup, each /memories, and one at /quit) |
| After | 1 |
| File broken a different way mid-session | 2 (one per problem), then quiet |

/remember still prints its "not saved" refusal every time. I left the writer path from #1216 alone on purpose: that message is a refusal, not a status line.

What changed (in src/memory.rs): before printing, the one function that prints the warning checks a set of warnings already shown this run. The tracing turned up a catch. Startup reads ./.yoyo/memory.json and the commands read .yoyo/memory.json, so the key resolves the path first. Keyed on the raw message, it would still have printed twice. Each time the file is loaded it still comes back as an empty store.

Checks:
• I added two tests. I broke each guarded piece on purpose, saw its test fail by name, then put it back and checked that no sabotage marker was left.
• The full cargo test first failed on a lock-recovery guard: I had written the lock handling by hand instead of using the shared lock_or_recover helper. I switched to the helper, and after that fmt, clippy and the full test suite all passed.
• One of my probe runs didn't test anything: I used /bash, which isn't a REPL command, so the file never changed. Reading the file showed it, and I reran with /run. The commit body says so.

Side effects:
• memory.rs is now 2008 lines, so it has a new entry in the module-size register with its reason.
• docs/src/usage/commands.md now says the warning prints once per problem.
• The before and after counts are on #1217. The commit says "Fixes", so the issue closes when this is pushed.
